e_Rector_of_Justin_1964_hardcover_dust_jacket.png)\n\n***The Rector of Justin*** is a 1964 [psychological 
fiction](/wiki/Psychological_fiction \"Psychological fiction\") novel by [Louis 
Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss \"Louis Auchincloss\") about the headmaster (or 
\"[rector](/wiki/Rector_(ecclesiastical) \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\") of a socially exclusive American 
[boarding school](/wiki/Boarding_school \"Boarding school\"). Over the decades, various narrators provide 
contrasting perspectives on rector Francis Prescott's charismatic personality and autocratic leadership style. 
Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils that [White Anglo-Saxon 
Protestant](/wiki/White_Anglo-Saxon_Protestants \"White Anglo-Saxon Protestants\") society—of which Prescott is a 
reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values.\n\nThe novel was a commercial hit and 
was warmly received by most contemporary critics. It was a finalist for the [Pulitzer Prize for 
Fiction](/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize_for_Fiction \"Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\") and the [National Book Award for 
Fiction](/wiki/National_Book_Award_for_Fiction \"National Book Award for Fiction\"), losing to [Shirley Ann 
Grau](/wiki/Shirley_Ann_Grau \"Shirley Ann Grau\")'s *[The Keeper of the House](/wiki/The_Keepers_of_the_House 
\"The Keepers of the House\")* and [Saul Bellow](/wiki/Saul_Bellow \"Saul Bellow\")'s 
*[Herzog](/wiki/Herzog_(novel) \"Herzog (novel)\")*, respectively. Its loss to *Herzog* marked [American literary 
fiction](/wiki/American_literature#Post-World_War_II_fiction \"American literature\")'s transition from realist 
[society novels](/wiki/Society_novel \"Society novel\") about the [American upper 
class](/wiki/American_upper_class \"American upper class\") to poetic prose, [avant-garde 
style](/wiki/Avant-garde_literature \"Avant-garde literature\"), and culturally diverse protagonists.\n\nUpon its 
release, *[Harper's Magazine](/wiki/Harper%27s_Magazine \"Harper's Magazine\")* called the book \"almost certainly
the finest work ever written about an American [preparatory school](/wiki/College-preparatory_school 
\"College-preparatory school\")\". It is often considered Auchincloss' greatest novel.\n\n## Synopsis\n\nThe story
is told through six narrators: [frame narrator](/wiki/Frame_story \"Frame story\") Brian Aspinwall, a teacher at 
Massachusetts [Episcopal](/wiki/Episcopal_Church_(United_States) \"Episcopal Church (United States)\") boarding 
school Justin Martyr (\"Justin\" for short; named for [the Christian figure](/wiki/Justin_Martyr \"Justin 
Martyr\")), who is asked to write a biography of its founder Francis Prescott; and writings and interviews from 
five people who knew him.\n\n### Brian Aspinwall\n\nIn 1939, Aspinwall begins teaching English at Justin. 
