»*](https://archives.newyorker.com/?i=1964-08-01#folio=076)\n\nFOR all its conventionality, its quiet stylistic 
grace, and its orotund moral sense, Louis Au-chincloss’s new nov-el, “The Rector of Justin” (Houghton Mifflin), is
a daring and ambitious book. At first glance, the chances it takes are small and safe. It deals principally with 
an eminent, long-lived (1860-1946) New England educator, it frets over such matters as honesty and goodness and 
godliness, and it moves steadily among the rich and/or wellborn. Yet it is almost always on thin ice. For it is an
attempt at a full-length portrait of a great man—which in fiction is an almost impossible task, generally 
resulting in a series of blinding closeups and fervent eulogies. It is also a novel about a school—an equally 
difficult subject, and one that has driven novelists to either brutal exorcising or genteel watercolors. And it is
centered on the seemingly insouciant rich and highborn, who have repeatedly caused writers to deify them (Henry 
James), seem proprietary about them (Edith Wharton), fawn over them (Dickens, Scott Fitzgerald), or simply 
lacerate them (Evelyn Waugh, Ronald Fir-bank). It does, by the way, appear odd, when one considers their 
extraordinary impress on affairs in general, that the rich are no longer fashionable subjects for the novel. But 
Au-chincloss meets nearly all these challenges, and though he himself could be the protagonist (old New York 
family, Groton, Yale, University of Virginia, law practice) of the very novel he has written, he views his 
background with steady wit and irony.\n\nThe hero of “The Rector of Justin s Francis Prescott, a New England 
aristocrat and a distant cousin of the great historian. His father is killed in the Civil War and his mother dies 
of grief soon after, so he is raised by aunts and uncles. By the time he reaches Oxford, by way of a New Hampshire
preparatory school, his character is firmly set. He is a short, broad, strong-faced boy who excels at both 
athletics and studies. He is a natural leader and he is deeply religious. He has already had a vision of what his 
lifework will be—to found a religious yet liberal New England prep school. This vision, though, melts away while 
he is at college, and then, tormented by a microscopic reading of the New Testament, he loses his religion. He 
takes a job with the New York Central in New York and falls in love with an outspoken Western beauty. He need only
reach out to acquire a wealthy and socially flawless career. But one night his father appears to him—as a ghost or
in a dream—and tells him that he must seek out God. After soul-wrestling of a sort that would not be possible 
today, he gives up his job and the girl and goes to divinity school, and in 1886 he founds Justin Martyr, an 
Episcopal boys’ boarding school, in a small town thirty miles west of Boston. He marries a classic Boston girl 
(hawk-nosed, amusing, intelligent) and has three daughters. By the turn of the century, he has become the lion of 
New England headmasters. Like many great leaders and teachers, he is a consummate ham and a thorough cosmopolite. 
He reads and speaks Greek and Latin fluently, he knows his Henry James and his wines and his Europe. He is rather 
a wit, and his sermons and lectures are handsomely modulated. His religion contains him, but he is also a just and
brilliant liberal. He is revered and feared by his students. His reputation …(truncated)"}, {"url": 
