$4.95.](https://s1.nyt.com/timesmachine/pages/1/1964/07/12/119048917_360W.png?quality=75&auto=webp&disable=upscale
)\n\n**L**OUIS AUCHINCLOSS, who is a practicing New York lawyer as well as a novelist, has gone on imperturbably 
writing traditional society novels despite the fact that this kind of novel and this society are both supposed to 
be dead. In his recent “Portrait in Brownstone,” he came brilliantly close to doing for the New York of today what
Edith Wharton (for whom his admiration is no secret) did for the New York of yesterday. In his new book, “The 
Rector of Justin,” he reaches for another order of magnitude and in so doing surpasses both Mrs. Wharton and 
himself. This is not only a passionately interesting, but a spiritually important study of the American character 
of, and for, our time.\n\nVoices will no doubt be raised—as they always are when a novel is centered upon what we 
now call the upper‐upper and upper‐middle classes—to protest that these classes no longer represent our country, 
that white Anglo‐Saxon Protestantism has ceased to be our distinguishing mark, and that to attempt to define 
American culture in such narrow terms is like trying to define an elephant in terms of its ears. It can be 
conceded that Baldwin's slums, Malamud's ghettos, O'Hara's suburbia, Nabokov's motel routes, O'Connor's parishes, 
and Faulkner's Yoknapatawpha County add up to a vastly preponderant America. Yet, like it or not, and justly or 
not, the preponderance of our leadership in government, the professions and business comes, still today, out of 
that social minority which preempts Mr. Auchincloss's field of vision.\n\nWhy this is so is no mystery. It is not 
a matter of divine right, nor even, necessarily, of divinely bestowed ability, but simply a matter of privilege. 
These are the people who can buy the private schooling which, despite all our efforts to close the gap between 
private and public education, is still the best we have.\n\n**S**INCE Mr. Auchincloss is fully aware of this, 
nothing could have been more logical than for him to take as the setting of his new novel the crucible in which 
the kind of men who most concern him are fired and tested and formed. That crucible is of course a New England 
Episcopal boarding school for boys. Named Justin Martyr by its founder and rector, the Rev. Francis Prescott, 
D.D., it is described as the leading school, as Dr. Prescott is described as the leading headmaster, in American 
secondary education.\n\nAny resemblance, however, between Justin Martyr and that other leading school called 
Groton, or between Dr. Prescott and that other leading headmaster, Dr. Endicott Peabody, is purely fanciful, as 
demonstrated by the fact that Dr. Peabody himself appears in the story briefly as a visiting preacher and is 
afterward summed up by his eminent colleague Dr. Pres‐ cott as “well equipped to train young men for the steam 
room of the Racquet Club.”\n\nAlthough Mr. Auchincloss focuses his book upon Dr. Prescott, he does not presume to 
enter directly the precincts of that awesome mind. It is through other eyes, most but not all of which gaze at the
