  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "local_ref": "e1",
      "text": "The reviewer calls The Rector of Justin “not only a passionately interesting, but a spiritually 
important study of the American character of, and for, our time.”",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["New York Times review language"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e2",
      "text": "The review states that in The Rector of Justin Auchincloss “reaches for another order of magnitude 
and in so doing surpasses both Mrs. Wharton and himself.”",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["comparative praise"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e3",
      "text": "The New York Times describes Justin Martyr as “the crucible in which the kind of men who most 
concern him are fired and tested and formed” and Dr. Prescott as “the leading headmaster in American secondary 
education.”",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["fictional setting description"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e4",
      "text": "The review anticipates objections to the focus on “upper-upper and upper-middle classes” and “white
Anglo-Saxon Protestantism,” but argues that this group “still supplies much of the nation’s leadership.”",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["social commentary in review"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e5",
      "text": "The New Yorker calls the novel “for all its conventionality, its quiet stylistic grace, and its 
orotund moral sense, … a daring and ambitious book.”",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["reviewer summary judgment"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "text": "The review notes that the book is “an attempt at a full-length portrait of a great man—which in 
fiction is an almost impossible task,” “also a novel about a school—an equally difficult subject,” and about “the 
seemingly insouciant rich and highborn,” and concludes that “Auchincloss meets nearly all these challenges.”",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["analysis of risks and success"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "text": "Kirkus describes the book as “a prismatic portrait of Dr. Prescott … as seen through the sometimes 
coincident, sometimes contradictory interpretations of his family and associates.”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["structural description"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "text": "Kirkus lists Brian Aspinwall’s sources, including “the memoir of a decadent dilettante, once 
Prescott's school friend and satellite,” “the verbal confidences of his third daughter—another Cordelia, whose 
love affair he had demolished,” and testimony from a former student turned trustee and that man’s son “whom he had
expelled in a particularly vindictive episode.”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["plot details"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "text": "The Kirkus review asks: “Leviathan? legend? charlatan? man of God or God among men? Prescott was 
many things to many people.”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["character evaluation"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "Kirkus comments that Prescott’s story “is told with a discretion which is more appropriate than 
animated,” but adds that it is “Auchincloss' first novel in some years which has gone beyond and below the 
superficial sheen of New York and Newport to provide a story of more substance.”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["mixed critical evaluation"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "The Kirkus review notes: “Its selection by the Book-of-the-Month club should extend a sphere of 
influence outside the ivy-and-moss covered purlieu of New England academia.”",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["Book-of-the-Month Club mention"],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "Robert M. Adams refers to The Rector of Justin as “an old fashioned ‘character study’ of the 
headmaster of an Episcopal boy's school.”",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
