'analysis'}, {'title': 'Conflicts and differing accounts', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Some reference sources differ in 
their description of Brian Aspinwall’s relationship to Justin Martyr. Character analyses and thematic essays 
describe him as a young English master who arrives at the school in 1939, aged about twenty‑seven, to replace a 
teacher who has left for military service, while a plot summary in a study‑guide context portrays him as a former 
student reflecting on his experiences.[12][13][19] The more detailed character and structure discussions, which 
emphasize his recruitment as a teacher and his age and circumstances, support viewing Brian primarily as a newly 
appointed master and biographer whose outsider‑insider position shapes the narrative.[12][16][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260627083630915755_3f11f956', '20260627083630927913_94cf3e5b', '20260627083630996656_5b9416e6', 
'20260627083630964304_f8cd8b27'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'There is also a discrepancy among sources 
regarding the identity of Prescott’s successor as rector. EBSCO’s character overview, Grokipedia’s synopsis, and 
an eNotes character list consistently identify Duncan Moore as the headmaster who succeeds Prescott in 1941 and 
introduces liberalizing reforms and modernization at Justin Martyr.[11][12][15] By contrast, one answer in a 
GradeSaver essay‑question section suggests that the trustees choose a young man named David to replace 
Prescott.[14] Given the weight and detail of the accounts naming Duncan Moore, and their alignment with thematic 
analyses of institutional reform in the novel, later discussions generally treat Moore as Prescott’s successor, 
while acknowledging that at least one secondary commentary presents an alternative view.[11][12][14][15]', 
